<parameternamelist>
<parametername>modType</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The type of mod to fix. <bold>Nullable</bold> </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>modName</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The name of the mod to fix to. <bold>Default</bold>: <computeroutput>""</computeroutput> </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>partFilter</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The filter for valid order indices <ndash/> empty for &quot;no filter&quot;. <bold>Default</bold>: empty </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>trackKeys</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para><verbatim>embed:rst 
The **caller&apos;s** key-tracking default, handed down by whatever is driving this edit
(:cpp:class:`GraphGroupEdit` passes its own ``trackKeys`` here) :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`
:raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

A subclass that has its own key-tracking setting decides how to combine the two;
:cpp:class:`RegFillMissing` treats this as a default it can turn *on* but not off, so
either side asking for tracking is enough. A subclass with no such setting simply
ignores it :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;` :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

**Default**: ``false``
</verbatim> </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>keysToTrack</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para><verbatim>embed:rst 
The **caller&apos;s** key-tracking key set, handed down the same way -- ``std::nullopt``
meaning &quot;every key&quot;, matching
:cpp:func:`IfContentPartColouring::updateColouring`&apos;s own convention :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`
:raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

**Default**: ``std::nullopt``
</verbatim></para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
